WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.442
Not all objectives are born equal.

2
00:00:02.442 --> 00:00:04.070
This is Priority-Constrained Descent.

3
00:00:06.650 --> 00:00:08.870
Deep learning rarely has a single objective.

4
00:00:09.317 --> 00:00:14.842
To prune a network, we pair the task loss with a sparsity penalty.

5
00:00:14.842 --> 00:00:17.392
Low-rank models add a rank penalty;

6
00:00:17.392 --> 00:00:19.092
quantization, a fidelity term;

7
00:00:19.092 --> 00:00:22.067
continual and federated learning, their own constraints.

8
00:00:22.517 --> 00:00:27.957
In every case, one loss is the deliverable, and the others only constrain how we get there.

9
00:00:27.957 --> 00:00:29.557
The objectives form a hierarchy.

10
00:00:30.633 --> 00:00:35.124
The usual recipe adds everything up with weights.

11
00:00:35.124 --> 00:00:45.227
But weights encode priority only implicitly, depend on the arbitrary scale of each loss, and need fragile sweeps.

12
00:00:45.667 --> 00:00:58.867
Gradient-based methods such as MGDA, PCGrad, CAGrad and FAMO combine the gradients directly instead, but they treat the objectives as symmetric peers.

13
00:00:59.300 --> 00:01:10.274
Swap which loss you call primary and which you call secondary, and MGDA, PCGrad and CAGrad return exactly the same update.

14
00:01:11.333 --> 00:01:14.043
They aim for Pareto stationarity:

15
00:01:14.043 --> 00:01:24.340
a point where no direction improves every objective at once, because the gradients can be combined to cancel out.

16
00:01:24.783 --> 00:01:27.590
But cancellation is not convergence.

17
00:01:27.590 --> 00:01:36.570
Here, the primary loss has two minima, and only the right one also satisfies the secondary.

18
00:01:37.007 --> 00:01:51.097
From three starting points, weighted sum, MGDA, PCGrad, CAGrad and FAMO all stall on the left, where the pull of one objective balances the push of the other.

19
00:01:51.547 --> 00:01:54.334
We call this a conflict equilibrium:

20
00:01:54.334 --> 00:01:58.515
the optimizer stops, but the secondary is never satisfied.

21
00:01:59.600 --> 00:02:02.253
Priority-Constrained Descent starts from a different principle:

22
00:02:02.253 --> 00:02:07.560
follow the primary gradient, and bend away from it only as much as needed.

23
00:02:08.000 --> 00:02:12.520
Here is the primary gradient, g-one, and the secondary gradient, g-two.

24
00:02:12.967 --> 00:02:19.344
Directions that make at least a tau-fraction of the best possible progress on the secondary form a half-space:

25
00:02:19.344 --> 00:02:20.407
the shaded region.

26
00:02:20.850 --> 00:02:24.975
If the primary gradient already lies inside, PCD leaves it untouched:

27
00:02:24.975 --> 00:02:28.350
the update is plain gradient descent on the primary.

28
00:02:28.800 --> 00:02:35.700
If not, PCD projects it onto the half-space, to the closest direction that still guarantees the secondary's progress.

29
00:02:36.150 --> 00:02:38.356
Formally, it's a tiny quadratic program:

30
00:02:38.356 --> 00:02:44.240
stay as close as possible to the primary gradient, subject to one progress constraint per secondary.

31
00:02:45.350 --> 00:02:49.890
As the geometry changes, PCD moves smoothly between three regimes.

32
00:02:50.333 --> 00:02:53.742
When the gradients agree enough, the constraint is inactive.

33
00:02:53.742 --> 00:02:58.288
When they agree, but not enough, PCD adds just enough of g-two.

34
00:02:58.288 --> 00:03:02.833
Under conflict it adds more, but never more than the constraint requires.

35
00:03:03.267 --> 00:03:07.373
The single knob tau sets how demanding the constraint is.

36
00:03:07.373 --> 00:03:11.479
At zero, PCD only asks not to hurt the secondary;

37
00:03:11.479 --> 00:03:16.817
as tau grows, the half-space recedes and the update leans toward the secondary.

38
00:03:17.900 --> 00:03:20.752
This makes PCD asymmetric by construction.

39
00:03:20.752 --> 00:03:32.638
The solution is always the primary gradient, with a coefficient of exactly one, plus non-negative multiples of the secondaries, each zero unless its constraint binds.

40
00:03:33.083 --> 00:03:36.335
So the labels matter.

41
00:03:36.335 --> 00:03:45.277
In the swap test, the symmetric methods returned the same update.

42
00:03:45.717 --> 00:03:55.007
Swap them for PCD, and the update changes, because PCD knows which objective is the deliverable.

43
00:03:56.083 --> 00:04:01.943
With more secondaries, each adds its own half-space, and PCD projects onto their intersection.

44
00:04:02.383 --> 00:04:05.979
For two or three objectives the solution is closed-form;

45
00:04:05.979 --> 00:04:10.373
in general it's a small K-by-K problem, negligible next to backpropagation.

46
00:04:10.817 --> 00:04:18.104
The intersection is empty only if the secondary gradients exactly cancel, a measure-zero event in high dimensions.

47
00:04:19.183 --> 00:04:20.295
One more ingredient:

48
00:04:20.295 --> 00:04:20.665
scale.

49
00:04:20.665 --> 00:04:26.963
In our pruning experiments, the regularizer's gradient is ten to a hundred times larger than the task's.

50
00:04:27.400 --> 00:04:35.085
PCD normalizes each gradient by a running average of its own norm, like Adam's second moment, but one scalar per objective.

51
00:04:35.085 --> 00:04:39.110
So tau means the same thing however the losses are scaled.

52
00:04:39.550 --> 00:04:42.593
Rescale the secondary across ten orders of magnitude:

53
00:04:42.593 --> 00:04:49.060
a fixed weighted sum is dragged across the whole Pareto front, while PCD's operating point stays put.

54
00:04:50.133 --> 00:04:54.213
Together, this gives three guarantees for the direction PCD computes.

55
00:04:54.650 --> 00:04:58.680
Every step makes a tau-fraction of first-order progress on every secondary.

56
00:04:59.117 --> 00:05:03.787
When the primary already does the job, PCD is exactly gradient descent.

57
00:05:04.233 --> 00:05:09.557
And the direction vanishes only when every objective is individually stationary:

58
00:05:09.557 --> 00:05:12.944
composite multi-stationarity, strictly stronger than Pareto stationarity.

59
00:05:12.944 --> 00:05:17.783
Conflict equilibria are not fixed points of the PCD direction.

60
00:05:18.233 --> 00:05:19.886
Back to our landscape:

61
00:05:19.886 --> 00:05:28.563
from all three starts, PCD passes the conflict equilibrium, crosses the barrier, and reaches the point where both objectives are minimized.

62
00:05:31.033 --> 00:05:38.543
In practice, a PCD step computes one gradient per objective, normalizes them, and solves the small projection.

63
00:05:38.983 --> 00:05:44.849
It rescales the result to the primary gradient's length and hands it to your usual optimizer.

64
00:05:44.849 --> 00:05:47.415
It's a drop-in replacement for the gradient.

65
00:05:48.483 --> 00:05:49.749
Now, the experiments.

66
00:05:49.749 --> 00:05:58.189
In structured pruning, the task loss is primary, and a group-lasso penalty that drives whole channels to zero is secondary.

67
00:05:58.189 --> 00:06:01.143
Removing them leaves a smaller, dense network.

68
00:06:01.583 --> 00:06:12.573
Across four architectures on CIFAR-10 and CIFAR-100, PCD stays above every multi-objective baseline at every compression target up to ninety-five percent.

69
00:06:13.017 --> 00:06:21.948
On ResNet-34 with CIFAR-100, PCD keeps seventy point nine percent accuracy with ninety percent of the parameters removed.

70
00:06:21.948 --> 00:06:25.422
The unpruned network scores seventy-three point three;

71
00:06:25.422 --> 00:06:27.407
the strongest baseline, forty-four.

72
00:06:27.850 --> 00:06:30.219
And tau works as a compression dial:

73
00:06:30.219 --> 00:06:38.680
turning it up trades accuracy for size, FLOPs and latency along a smooth curve, with most of the savings below tau equals zero point one.

74
00:06:39.750 --> 00:06:51.490
Against tuned pruning and constrained baselines at matched sparsity, PCD is better in twenty-eight of forty-seven comparisons in the compression regime, worse in one, and within noise in the rest.

75
00:06:51.933 --> 00:06:59.523
And the same tau costs a consistent amount of accuracy across configurations, unlike its raw-gradient counterpart, DBGD.

76
00:07:00.600 --> 00:07:09.710
With three objectives, the task loss, an l-one penalty for sparsity, and a nuclear norm for low rank, one tau still steers everything.

77
00:07:10.150 --> 00:07:21.774
On ResNet-34 with CIFAR-100, PCD keeps seventy-one percent accuracy with ninety-two point six percent of the weights at zero and an effective rank of twenty-four point five.

78
00:07:21.774 --> 00:07:27.370
The strongest baseline reaches forty-eight point seven percent, at rank one hundred eighty-five.

79
00:07:27.817 --> 00:07:36.497
Across the whole trade-off surface, PCD's dominated hypervolume is two point three to three point four times the strongest baseline's, in every configuration.

80
00:07:37.567 --> 00:07:42.063
On synthetic problems, conflict equilibria appear exactly where theory predicts:

81
00:07:42.063 --> 00:07:48.807
MGDA and CAGrad stall, with a trapped gradient that grows with the number of objectives.

82
00:07:49.250 --> 00:07:54.130
PCD drives the primary gradient to zero for every number of objectives we tested.

83
00:07:55.200 --> 00:07:59.600
Not all objectives are born equal, and your optimizer shouldn't treat them as if they were.

84
00:08:00.050 --> 00:08:07.276
PCD anchors to the objective that matters, bends only as much as needed, and gives you one interpretable dial.

85
00:08:07.276 --> 00:08:11.840
The paper, code and an interactive demo are on the project page.
